
int countDist(int arr[]) {
    int n = arr.length;                    // total number of elements
    HashSet<Integer> hm = new HashSet<>(); // set auto-ignores duplicates

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        hm.add(arr[i]);                    // add each element, dupes skipped
    }

    return hm.size();                      // output => count of distinct elements
}

/////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

**Backtrack for arr = {1, 2, 1, 3, 2}:**
```
i=0  arr=1  hm={1}        size=1
i=1  arr=2  hm={1,2}      size=2
i=2  arr=1  dup skipped   size=2
i=3  arr=3  hm={1,2,3}    size=3
i=4  arr=2  dup skipped   size=3

return 3

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

**Description:**

Counts distinct elements using a HashSet which automatically rejects duplicates. Simpler and faster than brute force. Time complexity is O(n) and space complexity is O(n).

---

**Test Cases:**
```
arr = {1, 2, 1, 3, 2}   => 3   // mixed duplicates
arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}   => 5   // all unique
arr = {1, 1, 1, 1, 1}   => 1   // all same
arr = {5}               => 1   // single element
arr = {}                => 0   // empty array
arr = {-1, -2, -1, 3}   => 3   // negative numbers
arr = {0, 0, 1, 2, 0}   => 3   // zeros as duplicates
```

